  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 242 AM MST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nSynoptically, our upper-level flow for the beginning of the week is \nexpected to remain fairly zonal until around Wednesday. Monday \nafternoon into the evening, an upper-level shortwave trough should \nmove east from the Colorado Rockies across the Tri-State region. \nCurrently, guidance (including the LREF, GFS, and NBM) show no \nprecipitation associated with this shortwave. For the early part of \nthe week, high temperatures are forecast to stay in the 50s and 60s, \nwith lows in upper 20s to lower 30s. Peak wind gusts across the \nmajority of the County Warning Area (CWA) should remain under 35 \nmph. \n\nBeginning Wednesday, the upper-level pattern changes quite a bit. \nGuidance (including the GFS, ECMWF, Can-NH, and LREF) points to a \nbroad trough advancing from Southern California and Baja region. As \nthe trough begins to move east, our upper flow should transition to \nsouthwesterly, with an accompanying 500 mb jet streak of 60-70 kts \npassing overhead. The main trough is expected to exit the region by \nSaturday, bringing a return to westerly or northwesterly flow. \n\nAt the surface, a low pressure system is expected to track just \nsouth of the CWA along the KS/OK border as it progresses east out of \nSoutheastern CO. Low temperatures during this system will likely \nrange the 20s to low 30s, along with highs in the 40s to low 50s. \nPrecipitation is expected with this system, with the LREF and NBM's \nProbability of Precipitation (PoPs) currently around 40-60%. Knowing \nhow the temperature spread is, seeing a mixed precipitation of rain \nand snow for the event is not unreasonable. There is around a 20% \nchance of minor travel impacts from winter weather through Thursday. \n\nUncertainty remains regarding the exact track and timing of this \nsystem. The GFS places the upper trough center farther north across \nthe far southern counties in the CWA, shifting the surface low \nnorthward and potentially increasing PoPs. Conversely the ECMWF and \nthe Can-NH keep the surface low farther south, which would lower the \nPoPs. While there are a few outliers choosing a faster or slower \nsolution, the majority of guidance is in agreement on Wednesday \nbeing the start time of the event. With all of that being said, \nthere is still a good chance that the Tri-State Area does see some \nprecipitation later this week. &&",
